1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,000
An autonomous coding agent just wrote this patch. A sighted reviewer scans it in seconds. A screen reader reads it one plus sign, one minus sign and one hunk header at a time, so the single line that changes an authentication check sounds exactly like everything around it.

2
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:58,000
This is Volcan, running on this machine. One button. It posts that patch to the Volcan API and gets back three things a person can use. The earcon plays first, so the verdict arrives before the sentence. This patch touches security, so the cue falls; a benign one rises. Then the spoken digest, which names the security-sensitive file ahead of the line detail instead of in line order. Then the same patch as six-dot Unicode Braille cells for a refreshable display. The engine label and the round trip are read off the response, measured in this browser.

3
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:32,000
The track question is whether the protocol is real. Volcan's Model Context Protocol server is written by hand to revision 2025-11-25 over Streamable HTTP, with no SDK. This button performs a real initialize and a real tools list call against the MCP endpoint, then prints the negotiated version, the session identifier taken from the response header, and the JSON Schema of all eleven tools. The conformance script runs the same handshake from a terminal.

4
00:01:32,000 --> 00:02:08,000
A high-consequence command is staged behind a single-use grant, bound by HMAC SHA-256 to the command's own payload hash and to a lease. The agent that proposed the action cannot release it. A human presents the grant, and the planner answers: allowlisted, would run git diff HEAD, executed is false. Nothing ran. That holds for the whole product. No file under src imports subprocess or any process spawn, and a test walks every module's syntax tree on each run to keep it that way.

5
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Everything shown here is checkable in one command. 254 automated tests. Eighteen of eighteen protocol conformance checks. And a readiness script that is allowed to fail, and does: no browser driver runs in continuous integration, and the deployed endpoint is an older commit than this one. There is no Alexa skill in this repository and no Bedrock model was called from this machine. The page says both on screen. Volcan is for the developer who reviews code by ear and by touch.
